WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:01.470
- [David] Hello, readers.

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.195
How do we know what is
true, and what isn't?

00:00:04.195 --> 00:00:08.526
My mama always told me, "Don't
believe everything you read.

00:00:08.526 --> 00:00:11.937
"Just because someone took the
time to write something down,

00:00:11.937 --> 00:00:14.277
"send it off to be typeset and designed

00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:16.667
"and printed in a book or
published on the internet,

00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:19.380
"doesn't mean they bothered
to tell you the truth."

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.530
You should be skeptical of
all informational writing

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.719
until the writer gives you
a reason to trust them.

00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:29.020
Honestly, you should be skeptical of me

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:31.000
until I give you a reason to trust me.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.460
Who am I, after all?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.231
Just some stranger on the internet.

00:00:34.231 --> 00:00:36.340
Well, that's a little unfair, actually.

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:38.560
You probably knew about Khan Academy

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.720
well before you watched this video,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.280
which means you have a sense
that the information we teach

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.238
is reputable or trustworthy.

00:00:45.238 --> 00:00:47.690
So, maybe I'm already at an advantage

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.330
for you trustworthinesswise,

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:51.510
because you associate
me with an institution

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.316
you already have good feelings about.

00:00:53.316 --> 00:00:55.940
When an author writes for
informational purposes,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:00:58.370
they want you to believe that
they're telling the truth,

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.834
but you can't just accept that on faith.

00:01:00.834 --> 00:01:03.410
No human being is so important

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.107
that they can't be bothered
to explain themselves.

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:07.240
Now, let's be clear,

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.640
that doesn't mean that
everyone you disagree with

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.617
personally owes you an explanation.

00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:14.380
It just means that you don't
have to swallow their arguments

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.850
hook, line, and sinker.

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.580
So, how do we move forward?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:18.970
We can't just go around

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.630
in a nightmarish swirl of misinformation,

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.236
never knowing whom to trust.

00:01:23.236 --> 00:01:24.410
That's no good.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.670
Society couldn't function.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.100
Imagine looking at the weather report

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:28.630
and saying, "I don't believe you!"

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.470
And then, walking outside
your home in shorts

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:32.257
when the forecast calls for snow.

00:01:32.257 --> 00:01:34.701
That would be terrible, for you.

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:36.817
The key is to ask yourself,

00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:39.359
"Why should you trust
the weather forecast?"

00:01:39.359 --> 00:01:40.350
Well,

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:44.099
forecasters are scientists,
specifically meteorologists.

00:01:44.099 --> 00:01:47.550
Their job is to study weather
patterns and make predictions.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.640
And that doesn't mean
they always get it right,

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.280
because they're making what
amounts to educated guesses

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:53.200
about the weather.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.480
But their expertise

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.320
and their access to weather
monitoring equipment

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.080
make them likelier to know
what the weather's going to do

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.050
than the average person would.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:04.600
And this is basically
the test you should bring

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.203
to all informational writing.

00:02:07.203 --> 00:02:11.460
Good readers look for clues
to establish trustworthiness,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.300
to establish that the author knows

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:14.418
what they're talking about,

00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:15.950
that they've done the research,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.168
that they're an expert worth listening to.

00:02:18.168 --> 00:02:20.030
Someone who hops on YouTube

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.507
and says, "Here's why your
teachers are lying to you

00:02:22.507 --> 00:02:24.310
"about the goblins who live in the moon,"

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:28.230
is someone that warrants some
skepticism, a little side-eye.

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:30.740
Also, how awesome would it be
if there were moon goblins?

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.430
And why would anyone want that information

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:33.370
to be kept secret?

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.160
Okay, when you look at a text,

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.963
you have to wrap your head
around three big things.

00:02:37.963 --> 00:02:40.767
One, the main idea or the claim.

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:42.900
What is the author trying to say,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.980
explain or convince you of?

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.355
Two, the evidence for that claim?

00:02:48.355 --> 00:02:52.670
What are the facts, details,
or other pieces of information

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.350
that tell us that that
claim is true or accurate?

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.306
And three, the reasoning.

00:02:58.306 --> 00:03:01.842
How the evidence prove
that the claim is true?

00:03:01.842 --> 00:03:05.330
Okay, so let's imagine that
this conspiracy guy on YouTube

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:08.120
is trying to make the
case about those goblins.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.594
Let's listen in on his argument.

00:03:10.594 --> 00:03:12.250
- [YouTuber] I firmly believe

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.470
that there is a colony of
goblins living inside the moon

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:18.100
and the government is keeping
this information from you.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:19.160
How do we know this?

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:19.993
Look at the moon!

00:03:19.993 --> 00:03:21.500
See that little squiggle?

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.260
You were taught that was
just a crater, but it isn't!

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.087
It's actually the tip of an
enormous goblin moon base.

00:03:27.087 --> 00:03:28.390
How do I know that?

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.328
The goblins told me so.

00:03:30.328 --> 00:03:32.050
I have a special phone line

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.930
from my house to the moon goblins.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.847
And through me, they can answer
any questions you've got.

00:03:36.847 --> 00:03:40.989
Our moon door is always
open, the goblins tell me.

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:43.038
- [David] All right, so, what's the claim?

00:03:43.038 --> 00:03:45.346
Goblins live inside the moon.

00:03:45.346 --> 00:03:46.862
What's his proof?

00:03:46.862 --> 00:03:48.810
What we think is a crater on the moon

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.983
is actually a goblin moon base.

00:03:50.983 --> 00:03:52.400
What's his reasoning?

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.492
What backs that up?

00:03:53.492 --> 00:03:55.660
He says the goblins told him so.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:57.500
Now, is that evidence?

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.928
Or is that just something he's telling us?

00:03:59.928 --> 00:04:04.030
Well, one way we can know
he's probably full of baloney

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.230
is that only he can talk
to the moon goblins.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.144
Right, he says he's got
a special phone line.

00:04:09.144 --> 00:04:11.714
They can answer questions through him.

00:04:11.714 --> 00:04:13.450
We can't talk to the goblins.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:15.780
We can't have access to
any of their information.

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:17.930
He's behaving like the information

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.140
is freely out there for
anyone, but it isn't.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:21.787
You have to go through him.

00:04:21.787 --> 00:04:25.406
So, can the stuff he's saying
be independently verified?

00:04:25.406 --> 00:04:28.190
That is to say, can anyone
other than this person

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.960
prove that there are moon goblins

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.430
or that that crater is a moon base?

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:35.730
Are there photos or
video from those goblins?

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:37.641
And if not, why not?

00:04:37.641 --> 00:04:40.049
This doesn't pass the smell test for me.

00:04:40.049 --> 00:04:43.700
There's proof, but only I
get to see it, is not proof.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:45.085
That's just made up.

00:04:45.085 --> 00:04:47.000
When it comes to making arguments,

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:49.092
you have to show your work.

00:04:49.092 --> 00:04:52.620
A good writer has to earn
the trust of their audience.

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:56.357
And a good reader has to make
a writer work for that trust.

00:04:56.357 --> 00:04:58.275
"What does that look
like?" I hear you asking.

00:04:58.275 --> 00:04:59.639
Excellent question.

00:04:59.639 --> 00:05:03.183
Let's take a look at a piece
that does its homework.

00:05:03.183 --> 00:05:04.607
Okay, let's read.

00:05:04.607 --> 00:05:06.457
"There are some conspiracy theorists

00:05:06.457 --> 00:05:08.707
"that claim that NASA's
moon landing missions

00:05:08.707 --> 00:05:11.197
"in the late 60s and 70s never happened,

00:05:11.197 --> 00:05:14.130
"despite the mountain of
evidence to the contrary.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.507
"They claim that the
photographs were faked,

00:05:16.507 --> 00:05:18.037
"the mission recordings were scripted,

00:05:18.037 --> 00:05:19.567
"and the moon rocks were nothing more

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.467
"than ordinary earth rocks.

00:05:21.467 --> 00:05:23.627
"Perhaps you are among
these conspiracists.

00:05:23.627 --> 00:05:25.147
"If so, let me prove to you

00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:27.402
"that the moon landings took place.

00:05:27.402 --> 00:05:29.657
"Perhaps you are skeptical
of the US government,

00:05:29.657 --> 00:05:31.387
"which was in the middle of the Cold War

00:05:31.387 --> 00:05:34.397
"with the Soviet Union and
a shooting war in Vietnam.

00:05:34.397 --> 00:05:36.757
"Perhaps you believe that
any information from NASA

00:05:36.757 --> 00:05:38.471
"is therefore suspicious.

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:39.667
"Fortunately for you,

00:05:39.667 --> 00:05:41.627
"there is plenty of third-party evidence

00:05:41.627 --> 00:05:44.509
"that Americans traveled
to and landed on the moon.

00:05:44.509 --> 00:05:47.617
"An observatory in the UK
that previously observed

00:05:47.617 --> 00:05:49.487
"the Russian launch of the Sputnik probe

00:05:49.487 --> 00:05:51.097
"used its radio telescope

00:05:51.097 --> 00:05:53.655
"to observe the 1969 moon landing.

00:05:53.655 --> 00:05:56.527
"The Bochum Observatory,
this one in Germany,

00:05:56.527 --> 00:05:58.347
"also confirms that mission.

00:05:58.347 --> 00:05:59.807
"Finally, present-day photos

00:05:59.807 --> 00:06:02.307
"from many different space
agencies around the world

00:06:02.307 --> 00:06:05.557
"have confirmed the presence
of footprints, rover tracks,

00:06:05.557 --> 00:06:07.889
"and the lunar rovers themselves.

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:10.577
"In order for a moon
landing to have been faked,

00:06:10.577 --> 00:06:13.077
"the rocket scientist
James Michael Longuski

00:06:13.077 --> 00:06:15.747
"estimates that more than 400,000 people

00:06:15.747 --> 00:06:17.877
"who worked on the
projects in some capacity

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:20.687
"would have had to keep
the conspiracy a secret.

00:06:20.687 --> 00:06:21.999
"In his 2006 book

00:06:21.999 --> 00:06:24.927
'The Seven Secrets of How to
Think Like a Rocket Scientist,'

00:06:24.927 --> 00:06:27.557
"Longuski suggests that given
how complicated it would be

00:06:27.557 --> 00:06:28.957
"to fake such a project,

00:06:28.957 --> 00:06:31.027
"it might actually be
easier to just go ahead

00:06:31.027 --> 00:06:33.095
"and do the moon landing
in the first place."

00:06:33.095 --> 00:06:35.660
So, if you don't believe
the US government,

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:38.587
this writer is saying, let's
look at some other sources.

00:06:38.587 --> 00:06:40.700
How do we know those sources are real?

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:42.790
Well, we have some names to look into

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.307
to independently verify it.

00:06:44.307 --> 00:06:46.067
We've got this rocket scientist.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:48.657
We have the name of
this German observatory.

00:06:48.657 --> 00:06:51.610
We could look into and
verify these statements.

00:06:51.610 --> 00:06:53.010
It's not just one guy

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.900
asserting that there's
goblins on the moon.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:58.640
It's someone saying here
are some non-NASA sources

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:01.283
that can confirm that
Americans landed on the moon.

00:07:02.201 --> 00:07:03.819
Be careful out there.

00:07:03.819 --> 00:07:07.370
It's a wild world of people
who wanna bend your brains

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:08.982
and turn you against your neighbors.

00:07:08.982 --> 00:07:11.340
Use your brain, your heart,

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:13.680
and your reading skills to fend them off,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.372
as you develop your principles.

00:07:15.372 --> 00:07:16.926
You've got this, readers.

00:07:16.926 --> 00:07:19.040
You can learn anything.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:19.873
David out.

